        "In modern design systems, a calendar month view is constructed as a deterministic 6-row by 7-column Matrix (representing exactly 42 date cells). Why does the matrix require exactly 42 cells? Consider the mathematical worst-case scenario: a 31-day month that begins on the very last day of the week (e.g., Saturday in a Sunday-first locale, or Sunday in a Monday-first locale). Day 1 occupies cell 6 of row 1. Rows 2, 3, 4, and 5 contain days 2 through 29. Days 30 and 31 spill over into row 6. If a calendar grid only rendered five rows (35 cells), that month would overflow and break the visual container layout. By standardizing on a fixed 6x7 matrix of 42 cells, the calendar maintains consistent height across all twelve months of the year, eliminating jarring layout shifts during month navigation.",
        "Generating this 42-cell matrix requires three sequential mathematical computations. First, Determine the First Day Offset: we query the day of the week on which the first day of the target month falls (`new Date(year, monthIndex, 1).getDay()`). If the locale specifies Monday as the first day of the week (ISO-8601), we adjust the offset using the formula `offset = (firstDayOfWeek - 1 + 7) % 7`.",
        "Second, Pad Leading Days from the Previous Month: if the target month starts on a Wednesday (offset 2 in a Monday-first calendar), the first two cells of the matrix (index 0 and 1) must be populated by the trailing days of the preceding month (e.g., October 30 and 31 preceding November 1). These cells are flagged with `isCurrentMonth: false` and styled with muted visual tokens so users understand they belong to the adjacent month.",
