        "A subtle edge case in calendar grid keyboard navigation is Handling Inactive and Disabled Dates. When a user presses ArrowRight and lands on a cell representing a disabled date (such as a company holiday or a past booking date), what should happen to virtual focus? Under WAI-ARIA guidelines, disabled cells MUST still receive virtual focus via aria-activedescendant so the screen reader can vocalize the date and its disabled status (e.g., \"Saturday, November 14, 2026, disabled, booking closed\"). If disabled cells were skipped entirely, the user would experience jarring spatial teleportation across the grid.",
        "Furthermore, calendar dialogs must manage Escape Key Dismissal and Focus Restoration cleanly. When a user presses Escape while navigating inside the calendar table, the popup must close immediately, and physical keyboard focus must be smoothly returned to the trigger button that originally opened the calendar. This prevents focus loss to document.body, ensuring keyboard users can continue navigating the form without interruption.",
        "In addition, keyboard navigation must provide graceful boundaries at Year Limits. If a financial application restricts historical ledger exploration to the past seven years, pressing PageUp or Shift+PageUp when reaching the earliest allowed boundary should not crash or produce invalid date calculations. The navigation engine checks date timestamps against minDate and maxDate limits, preventing navigation beyond permissible epoch thresholds."
